; A DYNAMIC MAP: built, inserted into, and read with a key nothing knows.
; inputs: 0 2 5 9
; expect: 0 0 20 20
;
; Reads key 2. With n inserts of i -> i*10 for i < n, that is 20 when n > 2 and
; absent otherwise -- so two of the four inputs MISS, which is the case worth
; checking on four hosts because absence is where they differ most:
;
;   Go          v, ok := m[k]
;   JavaScript  m[k], and undefined means absent
;   Java        m.get(k), and null means absent
;   windows     NO HOST MAP AT ALL -- open addressing over a `build` buffer,
;               written in Oroboros (emit/winmap.oro) and lowered before
;               reduction, so nothing below that file learns maps exist
;
; F2 REMOVES an obligation rather than adding one: the option makes the program
; say what happens when the key is absent, which is the only thing that can,
; since `k in dom m` is set membership and no fragment we have decides it.
;
; ABSENCE IS 0 AND NOT -1, which is a fact about the HARNESS rather than about
; maps: `win/fmt.print-int` prints a blank line for a negative number and is
; within its rights to (refinements.md §6b names exactly this as the gap
; inlining cannot reach). A -1 here made windows look like it had lost entries
; when it had computed them correctly and could not print them.
(def run (fn (n)
  (let (build-map 8 (fn (m)
         (loop ((m m) (i 0))
           (>= i n)  m
           else       (again (insert m i (* i 10)) (+ i 1)))))
    (fn (m) (case (m 2) (some v) v none 0)))))
